{% comment %}
  Renders the storefront landing page for D33's guest warranty order-lookup
  verification link (docs/ARCHITECTURE-MVP1.md D33,
  app/app/routes/apps.carat.warranty-order-verify.tsx,
  apps.carat.warranty-order-lookup.tsx). Reads `?token=` from the page URL
  and, ONLY on an explicit button press, POSTs it to
  `/apps/carat/warranty-order-verify` via `assets/warranty-order-verify.js`.
  On success, hands the returned order/line-item identifiers to the existing
  Warranty Claim Form (`sections/warranty-claim-form.liquid`) by navigating
  to it with `?order_id=`/`?line_item_id=` -- fields that form's own
  `assets/warranty-claim-form.js` (`prefillFromQueryString`) already reads.
  No new claim-submission code exists here; this page only gets the customer
  to that existing form with the right identifiers already filled in.

  ============================================================================
  WHY THIS PAGE MUST NOT SUBMIT ON LOAD -- READ BEFORE "SIMPLIFYING" THIS
  ============================================================================
  The verify route's own module comment (apps.carat.warranty-order-
  verify.tsx) explains why: the token is single-use, and a corporate email
  gateway's automated link-prefetch (Outlook Safe Links and similar,
  well-documented real-world behaviour) issuing a bare GET against a page
  that auto-fires the redemption would silently burn the customer's token
  before they ever open the email, permanently locking them out with no
  attacker involved. `assets/warranty-order-verify.js` only ever calls the
  verify endpoint from a real `click` handler on
  `[data-carat-warranty-verify-submit]` -- never from `DOMContentLoaded`,
  never from a bare page load. Do not "helpfully" wire that button to
  auto-click itself, and do not turn this into a `{% form %}` that a
  prefetcher's markup parser could otherwise be tricked into associating with
  autofill/auto-submit browser extensions -- it is a plain `<button
  type="button">`, not a submit input, for exactly that reason.

  TOKEN NEVER RENDERED. This file never echoes `token` into any attribute,
  href, or visible text -- there is nothing here for a template engine to
  even do that with, since Liquid has no access to the query string at all
  (only `assets/warranty-order-verify.js`, running in the browser, reads
  `window.location.search`). That script also scrubs the token out of the
  visible address bar via `history.replaceState` immediately on load, before
  rendering any state -- see its own header comment for why (the read-only
  Referer-leak risk on the SUBSEQUENT navigation to the claim-form page,
  which this section's whole job is to trigger).

  COPY STATUS -- PROVISIONAL, NOT OWNER-APPROVED. Every
  `templates.warranty_order_verify.form.*` locale string below is a DRAFT,
  same standing precedent as `sections/warranty-claim-form.liquid`'s own
  header comment and `snippets/bank-payment-checkout-dialog.liquid`'s: drafted
  from already-locked language in `docs/WARRANTY-CLAIMS.md` and this task's
  own route contracts rather than invented from scratch, shipped functional
  pending an owner pass rather than blocked on it. No return/warranty/
  final-sale POLICY term is restated here -- every panel that needs to say
  more than "we couldn't verify this" links out to the warranty claim page or
  a configurable contact page instead of asserting policy itself.

  NEXT-STEP LINKS ARE CONFIGURABLE, NOT GUESSED. This section does not
  hard-code a path to the warranty claim page or a support/contact page --
  both are `url` picker settings below (`warranty_claim_url`/`contact_url`)
  so the merchant can point them at whatever page/handle actually exists,
  without a code change. If left unset, `contact_url` simply omits the link
  (falls back to a plain, non-linked "contact us" sentence) rather than
  guessing a path that might not exist; `warranty_claim_url` falls back to
  `/pages/warranty-claim` in `assets/warranty-order-verify.js` only when
  unset, matching the backend's own literal-default convention for the
  companion `WARRANTY_ORDER_VERIFY_PATH` env var.

  RETRY IS INTENTIONALLY NOT OFFERED FOR MOST FAILURES. Once ANY HTTP
  response comes back from `/apps/carat/warranty-order-verify` -- success or
  failure -- `redeemWarrantyOrderLookupVerification` has already run its
  atomic `updateMany` (see that repository file's own code): the token is
  consumed the instant the row is claimed, strictly BEFORE the route even
  attempts to re-fetch the order from Shopify. That means a 404/500/502 that
  happens AFTER a successful redemption (the order-lookup step failing) still
  leaves the token spent -- clicking "Verify" again would only ever surface
  "already used", never a working retry. `assets/warranty-order-verify.js`
  therefore treats every completed HTTP response as terminal for this token:
  the Ready panel is replaced by the standalone Error panel, which has no
  retry control at all. The ONE exception is a `fetch()` promise rejection
  (a genuine network failure, no response ever received from the server),
  where the token was very likely never touched server-side at all -- that
  case shows an inline message INSIDE the still-visible Ready panel instead,
  and the same Verify button (left enabled) can simply be pressed again;
  no separate "retry" control exists anywhere in this file.

  Accepts (schema settings): heading (optional override), warranty_claim_url,
  contact_url.
{% endcomment %}
{{ 'component-warranty-order-verify.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

{%- style -%}
  .section-{{ section.id }}-padding {
    padding-top: {{ section.settings.padding_top | times: 0.75 | round: 0 }}px;
    padding-bottom: {{ section.settings.padding_bottom | times: 0.75 | round: 0 }}px;
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .section-{{ section.id }}-padding {
      padding-top: {{ section.settings.padding_top }}px;
      padding-bottom: {{ section.settings.padding_bottom }}px;
    }
  }
{%- endstyle -%}

{%- liquid
  # A plain "url" type section setting resolves directly to a String (or
  # blank) -- unlike a "video_url" type setting (a drop with id/type
  # properties) or a metafield (a drop needing a value property), so no
  # accessor suffix is used here. This tag block uses a hash line comment
  # rather than an HTML-comment-style multi-line tag, since the latter
  # breaks across newlines inside this tag -- every newline here starts a
  # new tag, and even the LITERAL TEXT of a multi-line comment tag's name,
  # written inside a hash comment on its own line, is enough to confuse the
  # parser into looking for a matching closer that was never opened as real
  # markup (found the hard way while writing this very comment).
  assign claim_url = section.settings.warranty_claim_url
  assign contact_url = section.settings.contact_url
-%}

{%- capture next_step_html -%}
  {%- if contact_url != blank -%}
    {{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.next_step_contact_html' | t: url: contact_url }}
  {%- else -%}
    {{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.next_step_contact_text' | t }}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

<div class="color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }} gradient">
  <div
    class="page-width page-width--narrow section-{{ section.id }}-padding carat-warranty-verify"
    data-carat-warranty-verify
    data-claim-url="{{ claim_url | escape }}"
  >
    <noscript>
      <p class="carat-warranty-verify__noscript">
        {{- 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.noscript_notice' | t -}}
      </p>
    </noscript>

    {%- if section.settings.heading != blank -%}
      <h2 class="title title-wrapper--no-top-margin {{ section.settings.heading_size }}">
        {{ section.settings.heading }}
      </h2>
    {%- else -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Visually hidden when unset -- `sections/main-page.liquid` (rendered
        first in this page's template) already shows the Shopify page's own
        title as an <h1>; see warranty-claim-form.liquid's identical
        treatment for the same reasoning.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <h2 class="visually-hidden">{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.heading' | t }}</h2>
    {%- endif -%}

    <p class="carat-warranty-verify__intro">
      {{- 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.intro' | t -}}
    </p>

    {%- comment -%}
      MISSING TOKEN -- shown only when `assets/warranty-order-verify.js`
      finds no `?token=` in the URL at all (e.g. the page was opened
      directly, not from the emailed link). No network request is made in
      this state; there is nothing to verify.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div
      id="WarrantyVerifyMissingToken"
      class="carat-warranty-verify__panel"
      role="alert"
      tabindex="-1"
      hidden
      data-carat-warranty-verify-missing-token
    >
      <p class="carat-warranty-verify__panel-heading">
        {{- 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.missing_token_heading' | t -}}
      </p>
      <p>{{- 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.missing_token_body' | t -}}</p>
      <p class="carat-warranty-verify__next-step">{{ next_step_html }}</p>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      READY -- a token is present but not yet redeemed. The submit control is
      a plain `<button type="button">`, not inside a `{% form %}`/submit
      input -- see this file's header comment on why. Nothing here fires
      automatically; `data-carat-warranty-verify-submit` only reacts to a
      real `click`.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div id="WarrantyVerifyReady" class="carat-warranty-verify__panel" hidden data-carat-warranty-verify-ready>
      <h3 id="WarrantyVerifyReadyHeading" class="carat-warranty-verify__panel-heading" tabindex="-1">
        {{- 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.ready_heading' | t -}}
      </h3>
      <p>{{- 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.ready_body' | t -}}</p>
      <button type="button" class="button" data-carat-warranty-verify-submit>
        {{- 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.verify_button' | t -}}
      </button>
      <p
        id="WarrantyVerifyStatus"
        class="carat-warranty-verify__status form-status"
        role="status"
        aria-live="polite"
        hidden
        data-carat-warranty-verify-status
      ></p>
      {%- comment -%}
        NETWORK FAILURE ONLY -- lives inside the Ready panel, not the
        terminal Error panel below, precisely because a `fetch()` promise
        rejection (no response ever received) is the ONE case where the
        token was very likely never touched server-side -- see this file's
        header comment. The customer stays on this same panel and can simply
        press Verify again; no separate "retry" control is needed.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div
        id="WarrantyVerifyReadyError"
        class="carat-warranty-verify__panel-error"
        role="alert"
        tabindex="-1"
        hidden
        data-carat-warranty-verify-ready-error
      >
        <p class="carat-warranty-verify__panel-heading" data-carat-warranty-verify-ready-error-heading></p>
        <p data-carat-warranty-verify-ready-error-body></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      SUCCESS -- the order was verified. The customer picks which line item
      the claim is for, then Continue navigates (a normal page load, not a
      fetch) to the Warranty Claim Form page with `?order_id=`/
      `?line_item_id=` already filled in -- see this file's header comment.
      Line-item radios are built entirely by JS from the server's response;
      nothing about a specific order/item is known at theme-render time.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div id="WarrantyVerifySuccess" class="carat-warranty-verify__panel" hidden data-carat-warranty-verify-success>
      <h3 id="WarrantyVerifySuccessHeading" class="carat-warranty-verify__panel-heading" tabindex="-1">
        {{- 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.success_heading' | t -}}
      </h3>
      <p id="WarrantyVerifySuccessIntro" data-carat-warranty-verify-success-intro></p>

      <fieldset class="carat-warranty-verify__fieldset">
        <legend class="carat-warranty-verify__legend">
          {{- 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.items_question_legend' | t -}}
        </legend>
        <div id="WarrantyVerifyItems" class="carat-warranty-verify__radio-group" data-carat-warranty-verify-items></div>
        <small
          id="WarrantyVerifyItemsError"
          class="carat-warranty-verify__field-error form__message"
          role="alert"
          hidden
          data-carat-warranty-verify-items-error
        >
          <span class="svg-wrapper">{{- 'icon-error.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}</span>
          <span>{{- 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.error_choose_item' | t -}}</span>
        </small>
      </fieldset>

      <button type="button" class="button" data-carat-warranty-verify-continue>
        {{- 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.continue_button' | t -}}
      </button>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      ERROR -- always terminal for this token (network failures never reach
      this panel; see the inline error inside the Ready panel above for
      that case). Only the heading/body text are set by JS from a small
      error-code lookup -- the "next step" line below is the SAME static,
      server-rendered markup as the missing-token panel's, reused as-is,
      since every terminal error's next step is identical ("contact us");
      only which problem occurred differs. No retry control -- see this
      file's header comment on why one would never help here.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div
      id="WarrantyVerifyError"
      class="carat-warranty-verify__panel"
      role="alert"
      tabindex="-1"
      hidden
      data-carat-warranty-verify-error
    >
      <p id="WarrantyVerifyErrorHeading" class="carat-warranty-verify__panel-heading" data-carat-warranty-verify-error-heading></p>
      <p id="WarrantyVerifyErrorBody" data-carat-warranty-verify-error-body></p>
      <p class="carat-warranty-verify__next-step">{{ next_step_html }}</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  // Locale-driven strings for this page only -- deliberately kept local to
  // this section rather than added to layout/theme.liquid's global
  // `window.*Strings` blocks (see that file's own existing pattern for
  // `warrantyClaimStrings` etc.): this section renders on exactly one page,
  // so a page-scoped global costs nothing and avoids touching the
  // shared, frequently-edited layout file for an unrelated feature.
  window.warrantyOrderVerifyStrings = {
    error_summary_by_code: {
      invalid_token: {
        heading: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.error_invalid_token_heading' | t }}`,
        body: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.error_invalid_token_body' | t }}`,
      },
      token_expired: {
        heading: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.error_token_expired_heading' | t }}`,
        body: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.error_token_expired_body' | t }}`,
      },
      token_already_used: {
        heading: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.error_token_already_used_heading' | t }}`,
        body: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.error_token_already_used_body' | t }}`,
      },
      order_unresolvable: {
        heading: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.error_order_unresolvable_heading' | t }}`,
        body: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.error_order_unresolvable_body' | t }}`,
      },
      service_unavailable: {
        heading: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.error_service_unavailable_heading' | t }}`,
        body: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.error_service_unavailable_body' | t }}`,
      },
      network: {
        heading: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.error_network_heading' | t }}`,
        body: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.error_network_body' | t }}`,
      },
    },
    verifying_status: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.verifying_status' | t }}`,
    // `order`/`title`/`quantity` are NOT known at theme-render time -- they
    // come from the verify endpoint's runtime response. This passes a
    // square-bracket placeholder (`[order]`, etc.) as the interpolation
    // argument -- exactly `layout/theme.liquid`'s existing
    // `cart_quantity_error_html` / `quantity: '[quantity]'` precedent, and
    // for the SAME reason: passing the double-curly-brace interpolation
    // marker itself back in AS the argument value does not parse -- Liquid's
    // own output-tag parser rejects that literal marker appearing a second
    // time nested inside the same output tag (confirmed by `shopify theme
    // check` on a first attempt at this exact line, and again when this very
    // explanation quoted the marker directly in a comment on this line --
    // do not requote it here either). The translation VALUE in
    // en.default.json still legitimately uses Shopify's own double-curly
    // interpolation syntax for these names; only the ARGUMENT this call
    // supplies is bracket-style, and `assets/warranty-order-verify.js`
    // finds-and-replaces that same `[order]`/`[title]`/`[quantity]` text
    // once the real value is known (see `formatItemOption`/`showSuccess`
    // there).
    success_intro_html: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.success_intro_html' | t: order: '[order]' }}`,
    item_option_with_quantity: `{{ 'templates.warranty_order_verify.form.item_option_with_quantity' | t: title: '[title]', quantity: '[quantity]' }}`,
  };
</script>
<script src="{{ 'warranty-order-verify.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Warranty order verify",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "section",
  "disabled_on": {
    "groups": ["header", "footer"]
  },
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading",
      "label": "Heading"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "heading_size",
      "options": [
        { "value": "h2", "label": "Small" },
        { "value": "h1", "label": "Medium" },
        { "value": "h0", "label": "Large" }
      ],
      "default": "h1",
      "label": "Heading size"
    },
    {
      "type": "color_scheme",
      "id": "color_scheme",
      "label": "Color scheme",
      "default": "scheme-1"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Links"
    },
    {
      "type": "url",
      "id": "warranty_claim_url",
      "label": "Warranty claim form page",
      "info": "Where the customer continues after verification. If left blank, defaults to /pages/warranty-claim."
    },
    {
      "type": "url",
      "id": "contact_url",
      "label": "Contact / support page (optional)",
      "info": "Linked from every \"couldn't verify\" message. If left blank, that text is shown without a link."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Section padding"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding_top",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "label": "Top padding",
      "default": 36
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding_bottom",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "label": "Bottom padding",
      "default": 36
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Warranty order verify"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
